design:
v5 proposer:
(1) Per-code call. One call per diagnosis code with at least 2 distinct proposal-half failed items, plus either at least 4 check-half failed items with that code (v3 semantics) or a later judge-sized trigger (v4 semantics). Applied to v3, this gives 27 calls instead of 10, and none for the 8 unsupported codes.
(2) Balanced evidence:
- up to 12 target-code failures (one run per item, seeded);
- up to 6 failures of the declared opposite code (SearchQA D1<->D2, D4<->D5; OfficeQA D2<->D3, D1<->D5; LiveMath D1<->D2, D3<->D4; SpreadsheetBench D3<->D4, D2<->D6; ALFWorld D3<->D4, D1<->D2);
- up to 6 always-correct items, stratified on a code-relevant feature.
Every shown target item carries the code, so evidence_other_code cannot occur. A LiveMath meta-option call must see D2 failures (meta option wrongly chosen).
(3) Template as slots: when (one observable test, at most 200 characters), do (at most 300), otherwise (at most 120). Code joins them as "When <when>: <do>. Otherwise, <otherwise>.", which guarantees the form and the 600-character limit. A worked example on a made-up task kind (thousands vs millions) shows one good rule and two bad ones (a global restatement; a task fact).
(4) Explicit constraints, checked by code:
- not already said ("covered" quotes the card or Skill);
- no task facts; examples must be invented placeholders;
- no 8-word copy, no reference answer or multi-word answer phrase, no reference number with 3 or more significant digits, no option text;
- no answer-distribution steering;
- support of at least 4 failing unseen items (MIN_TRIGGER);
- character limits and the Skill's remaining bytes (20,000 minus the parent);
- evidence ids only from shown items.
(5) Placement and transfer are removed from the proposer.
(6) Row-level validation with at most 2 re-asks that carry the concrete fix. A reply is never rejected for one field.
(7) 2 samples per code, keeping the union. A merge-time cross-code consistency check.
v5.1 additions from the pilot (untested): the proposer states the share of tasks where its condition holds (aim below 0.3; above 0.5 is treated as global); one test per condition (two tests make two rules); conditions on the solver's own draft are allowed; the risks list is dropped. The tested prompt is /data/feasibility/D13/run2/scripts/v5prompt.py (system SHA-256 prefix 16c8b552a2e88dea).

cost_estimate:
Measured: this pilot used 175,147 GLM-5.3 tokens. The proposer calls cost 22-29k tokens each; the 97-item applicability pass about 31k; the second rating 13.5k.
Estimated for v5 per chain on v3-sized stages:
- proposer: 11-16 per-code calls x 25-45k = 0.3-0.7M GLM tokens (v3 measured: I-ds 476,736 and E-ds 272,733 analyst tokens);
- applicability judging: about 31k per SearchQA code pass; 50-150k for OfficeQA and SpreadsheetBench, whose conditions need page or workbook context;
- total about 1-2M GLM tokens per chain, under 2% of the 139M v3 run.
Optional next screen pilot: the 5 limit-passing pilot rules plus v3 E-ds s1-r1, about 6 arms x 20 items x 4 runs x 3.4k, roughly 1.6M deepseek tokens.

recommendation:
Adopt in v4/v5:
- the per-code proposer with balanced evidence (target, opposite, always-correct);
- slot-template conditional rules joined by code, with explicit constraints enforced by code;
- row-level re-ask carrying the concrete fix (at most 2);
- no placement or transfer field in the proposer;
- 2 samples per code;
- the v5.1 additions (share estimate, one test per condition, conditions on the solver's own draft);
- triggers sized by a voted applicability judge over the whole check half, not by code.
Expected gain (estimate): prepare-stage survival rises from 28 of 49 to about 37-40 of 49, the 3 wasted re-asks and the within-reply contradictions disappear, and harm exposure shrinks (pilot: 3 of 72 always-correct items vs 72 of 72 for a global rule).
Do not expect more accepted rules from this alone. Pair it with D5/D8 (noise and power on judge-sized panels) and D9 (headroom), and before any chain run the cheap screen pilot of the conditional pilot rules against v3 E-ds s1-r1 to test whether lower C2 harm comes without lost C1 gain. Never report proposal-quality improvements as method-effect evidence.
